WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:04.800
Hey Wisecrack, Jared again.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.400
While there are plenty of recent films that
have disappointed us – today we decided

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:15.700
to go back in time to Summer 2003- a time
when 14 year old me was waiting in line 6

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:20.910
hours early to catch a glimpse at what would
be one of the biggest disappointments of my

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:21.910
fanboy life.

00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:25.589
"Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on The
Matrix Reloaded: What Went Wrong?

00:00:25.589 --> 00:00:28.480
And in case you’ve been living under a rock,
spoilers ahead.

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:33.700
Part of the reason why the original Matrix
worked so well was that it coupled deep philosophical

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:38.940
themes with a tight structure, influenced
heavily by Joseph Campbell’s idea of the

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:39.950
Monomyth.

00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:44.050
In The Hero with a Thousand Faces, Campbell
explains that a hero’s journey can be broken

00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:49.430
down into three phases: “separation from
the world, a penetration to some source of

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:52.170
power, and a life-enhancing return”.

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:56.540
This is a classic structure – one that’s
been used time and time again.

00:00:56.540 --> 00:01:01.180
Slavoj Zizek, I mean… Dan Harmon, writer
of Wisecrack favorite Rick and Morty, says

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:06.509
that this model is “hardwired into [our]
nervous system, and trust that in a vacuum,

00:01:06.509 --> 00:01:10.200
raised by wolves, [our] stories would follow
this pattern” (6).

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:13.659
But where does a story go once this cycle
is complete?

00:01:13.659 --> 00:01:17.850
Once the protagonist has become, in Campbell’s
words: “Master of Two Worlds” (196), and

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:20.200
in Neo’s case, freakin Superman?

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:24.310
This is the problem the Wachowski’s faced
in writing the Matrix Reloaded – and it’s

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:26.100
a pretty difficult problem.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:30.450
Once your character has become all-powerful,
how do you craft a meaningful story arc with

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:33.880
believable internal and external conflict?

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:38.369
To their credit, the Wachowskis’ solution
is pretty smart – at least in theory.

00:01:38.369 --> 00:01:42.120
While they can’t change the fact that Neo
is the love child of God and Kryptonite, they

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:49.729
can tweak the prophecy that gives him these
powers in the next movie.

00:01:49.729 --> 00:01:53.560
And this forms the central plot of the Matrix
Reloaded.

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:58.060
Over the course of the film, Neo and the audience
slowly realize that the prophecy is actually

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:05.340
just another form control.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:09.030
All of Neo’s powers aren’t evidence of
him transcending the system.

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:13.980
His ability to do these things are just another
part of the system.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:18.489
In other words, Neo isn’t breaking free
from the Matrix in any meaningful way, but

00:02:18.489 --> 00:02:21.500
rather playing a predetermined role in a larger
system.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:22.500
Or as the Architect [very cryptically] says:
“You are the eventuality of an anomaly,

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:23.500
that despite my sincerest efforts, I have
been unable to eliminate from what is otherwise

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:24.500
a harmony of mathematical precision.

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:25.500
While it remains a burden assiduously avoided,
it is not unexpected and thus, not beyond

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:26.500
a measure of control.”

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:27.500
Which brings us to the central problem of
the film: action.

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:30.590
When we say action, we don’t mean over the
top fight scenes, which there are plenty of.

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:34.950
Instead, what we’re talking about here is
dramatic action – or any choice that a character

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:39.500
makes to advance their goal and the overall
plot of the film.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:41.659
These don’t have to be big, weighty choices.

00:02:41.659 --> 00:02:47.200
They can be as small as Luke deciding to follow
R2D2 on his mission to find Obi Wan.

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:51.269
And, when it comes down to it, there just
isn’t much dramatic action in the Matrix

00:02:51.269 --> 00:02:52.269
Reloaded.

00:02:52.269 --> 00:02:56.780
Add in the fact that all of Neo’s “choices”
are just an illusion created by a larger system

00:02:56.780 --> 00:03:01.800
of control, and you start to realize that
there’s actually no dramatic action here.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.510
Zero, zip, none.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:09.900
Instead, we're bogged down by lengthy, philosophically
confused monologues about determinism.

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:13.530
Determinism loosely believes that through
cause and effect, every moment in the future

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:15.849
is dictated by a moment in the past.

00:03:15.849 --> 00:03:18.239
Think of it like watching dominos fall.

00:03:18.239 --> 00:03:24.659
If you know which domino falls first, you
can be certain which one falls next, ad infinitum.

00:03:24.659 --> 00:03:27.990
The Matrix Reloaded takes this philosophy
and runs with it.

00:03:27.990 --> 00:03:32.050
The opening scene is actually from the film’s
closing moments, hammering home that the events

00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:35.329
of the past have already cemented the film’s
ending.

00:03:35.329 --> 00:03:40.290
In the scene, Neo dreams of Trinity kicking
ass with a bike helmet before being shot.

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:44.480
Factor in that the Matrix has restarted six
times already and you begin to realize that

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:48.939
the film’s ending isn’t just predetermined,
but that the whole world has been literally

00:03:48.939 --> 00:03:50.540
stuck in a loop.

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:54.409
The punch clock in the guards’ office strikes
midnight, showing the literal completion of

00:03:54.409 --> 00:04:00.030
a circle and the seamless transition from
one day’s end to another’s beginning.

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:04.159
And the Key Maker, while explaining his plan
to infiltrate the tower, says that they have

00:04:04.159 --> 00:04:11.069
exactly 314 seconds for Neo to reach the Architect
– a conspicuously exact amount of time,

00:04:11.069 --> 00:04:13.610
which happens to be the first three digits
of pi.

00:04:13.610 --> 00:04:17.850
Side note: pi also shows up in other places
too, like being spray painted on the wall

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:19.709
outside of the Oracle’s apartment.

00:04:19.709 --> 00:04:24.650
These images are constant reminders that Neo
isn’t blazing a new path, but rather retreading

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:27.009
a circle for the 6th time.

00:04:27.009 --> 00:04:31.650
Neo awakes from this dream, worried as only
Keanu Reeves can possibly emote.

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:36.199
Importantly, there are no choices made in
this opening, except for Neo choosing to admit

00:04:36.199 --> 00:04:38.310
he doesn't know what to do.

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:40.670
Where is our first point of dramatic action,
you ask?

00:04:40.670 --> 00:04:45.110
The first moment when a character makes a
decision to advance his or her goals and the

00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:46.110
plot?

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:47.110
Well, that’s a long ways off.

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:50.000
In fact, it’s another 39 minutes off.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:54.660
Almost the whole first hour of the film involves
Morpheus and crew playing politics while they

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:56.880
seek the Oracle.

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:02.160
This gives us such memorable moments as pilgrims
asking Neo to bless their objects like Jesus,

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:07.910
future tribal rave scene 2.0, and a prolonged
conversation between Neo and Councillor Hamman

00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:17.010
that makes sure the audience knows that the
movie is going to question Neo’s agency.

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:19.729
So when does the dramatic action actually
start here?

00:05:19.729 --> 00:05:24.900
It’s not until Neo finally locates the Oracle
44 minutes into the movie that the protagonist

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:27.599
is given their quest and starts their journey.

00:05:27.599 --> 00:05:31.800
Compare this to Star Wars, where Luke receives
Leia’s message at about the twenty minute

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:32.800
mark.

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:37.470
And when this moment finally comes, in typical
Matrix Reloaded style, the film decides to

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:45.410
beat the living crap out of us with its weighty,
deterministic philosophy first.

00:05:45.410 --> 00:05:50.160
Neo wonders what he should do, but the Oracle
reminds Neo and viewers that it doesn’t

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:55.800
matter – Neo’s already made his choice.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:06:06.690
That’s right, in case we were hoping that
Neo would make a choice here, we were wrong.

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:11.940
So, to recap, the inciting incident is just
a bunch of philosophical exposition that reminds

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:15.890
us that Neo has no agency at all.

00:06:15.890 --> 00:06:17.360
Fun.

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:25.530
But what about Neo’s quest?

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:35.900
Yeah, Neo’s journey is literally a glorified
fetch quest.

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:40.009
You know, Those obnoxious, shitty gathering
quests that has been ruining RPG’s for thirty

00:06:40.009 --> 00:06:41.160
years now.

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:44.169
But at least those in quests, we know why
we’re starting them.

00:06:44.169 --> 00:06:46.949
All Neo’s left with is vague instructions.

00:06:46.949 --> 00:06:47.949
But don’t worry.

00:06:47.949 --> 00:06:51.640
The Wachowski’s, sensing their audience
might be getting annoyed now, decide to add

00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:53.970
in an action scene with Agent Smith.

00:06:53.970 --> 00:07:11.410
And surprise surprise, Smith delivers a monologue
on determinism before attacking Neo.

00:07:11.410 --> 00:07:19.190
Yep, while Neo might think he’s free, Smith
is trying to tell him that he’s only bound

00:07:19.190 --> 00:07:24.330
by a larger system of cause and effect – just
like Councilor Hamaan and the Oracle teased

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:25.330
earlier.

00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:29.509
Instead of having this deterministic theme
grow organically through Neo’s actions,

00:07:29.509 --> 00:07:33.110
The Matrix Reloaded decides to just tell us
it straight up.

00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:35.050
Over and over again.

00:07:35.050 --> 00:07:38.919
Then we have another action sequence that
demonstrates Neo’s Godlike power, a scene

00:07:38.919 --> 00:07:41.409
that doesn’t advance the plot at all.

00:07:41.409 --> 00:07:45.319
Adding insult to injury, this fight scene
goes on for well over 6 minutes and looks

00:07:45.319 --> 00:07:48.509
like it was ripped directly from a Dynasty
Warriors game.

00:07:48.509 --> 00:07:52.409
An hour into the film, and the plot finally
begins to move.

00:07:52.409 --> 00:07:55.890
Neo and crew meet with the Merovingian to
haggle over the Key Maker.

00:07:55.890 --> 00:07:59.500
The Merovingian is a reference to the Merovingian
Order of the Holy Grail, which supposedly

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:03.379
guarded a book detailing the location of the
Holy Grail.

00:08:03.379 --> 00:08:07.139
Keeping with the film’s Christian symbolism,
the Key Maker is the book here, while the

00:08:07.139 --> 00:08:08.970
Source is the Grail.

00:08:08.970 --> 00:08:29.050
But this wouldn’t be the Matrix Reloaded
without another unnecessary speech on determinism.

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:33.580
To prove his point, in what might go down
as the most ridiculous scene in an already

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:38.610
ridiculous movie, our French friend here to
decides to send a girl a piece of cake designed

00:08:38.610 --> 00:08:40.520
to make her orgasm.

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:42.880
Yeah, that just happened.

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:47.910
This is now the fourth ramble sesh teasing
to Neo that he’s just a puppet on strings.

00:08:47.910 --> 00:08:55.920
But the Merovingian decides to withhold the
Key Maker.

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:00.101
Luckily for Neo and company – and unluckily
for audiences – the group doesn’t have

00:09:00.101 --> 00:09:04.870
to think of a clever solution to retrieve
the Key Maker, which is, ya know, what protagonists

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:06.980
usually do when faced with an obstacle.

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:10.610
Instead, the Merovingian’s jilted lady friend,
Persephone, offers him up in exchange for

00:09:10.610 --> 00:09:18.620
a kiss with Neo.

00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:22.750
If you think that’s male fantasy for you,
know that the script actually described her

00:09:22.750 --> 00:09:27.530
as “sex and death squeezed into a woman’s
business suit made of latex” (98).

00:09:27.530 --> 00:09:28.530
Yeah.

00:09:28.530 --> 00:09:32.930
Then begins the movie’s largest action sequence
– taking up 22 minutes, or the length of

00:09:32.930 --> 00:09:34.750
an entire television show.

00:09:34.750 --> 00:09:37.760
And this scene doesn’t make a whole lot
of sense, if you think about it.

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:42.510
If Neo is just playing into the hands of the
Architect like all his other past incarnations,

00:09:42.510 --> 00:09:47.450
why is it necessary that the Agents chase
the Key Marker?

00:09:47.450 --> 00:09:52.740
After all, if they delete him, then Neo can’t
reach the Source and reintegrate his code.

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:57.260
Which means that the Matrix is super screwed,
because Agent Smith is going rogue and there’s

00:09:57.260 --> 00:09:58.769
nothing to stop him.

00:09:58.769 --> 00:10:03.860
So BOTH of the film’s biggest action set
pieces serve no purpose in the narrative.

00:10:03.860 --> 00:10:07.589
Factor in how much time has been wasted talking
about determinism, and you realize that this

00:10:07.589 --> 00:10:11.140
film can’t have spent more than twenty minutes
advancing the plot.

00:10:11.140 --> 00:10:14.550
But there’s not much time to think about
this, because the film moves into its third

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:18.720
plot point well past the ninety minute mark:
the journey to the Source.

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:22.959
This is supposed to be the climax of the film,
but it really doesn’t feel like it.

00:10:22.959 --> 00:10:26.910
For starters, we already know what happens
when the group infiltrates the tower.

00:10:26.910 --> 00:10:30.920
Despite Neo telling Trinity to stay out of
the Matrix, we know that she’ll go in anyway

00:10:30.920 --> 00:10:33.399
and get herself shot as a result.

00:10:33.399 --> 00:10:37.420
Meanwhile, once Neo reaches the Source, he
doesn’t come up against some insurmountable

00:10:37.420 --> 00:10:41.990
foe who forces him to become one with himself
and the universe, partially because this already

00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:43.399
happened in the first film.

00:10:43.399 --> 00:10:47.089
No, we’re given typical Matrix Reloaded
fare again.

00:10:47.089 --> 00:10:52.520
Instead of fulfilling dramatic action, we
get MORE exposition- a big ol information

00:10:52.520 --> 00:10:59.300
dump.

00:10:59.300 --> 00:11:03.720
The Architect explains the role of the prophecy
and how Neo must eventually surrender his

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:08.350
code to the Matrix mainframe like his five
predecessors have done before him, thus saving

00:11:08.350 --> 00:11:10.110
Zion from complete destruction.

00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:15.300
If there’s any hope for Neo making a choice
here, rest assured – the film is hellbent

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:37.760
on taking away his agency.

00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:42.519
The Architect even says that Neo was specifically
“designed” to feel love more than his

00:11:42.519 --> 00:11:43.600
predecessors.

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:47.480
And just to make sure the film really hammers
the point home, when Neo is presented with

00:11:47.480 --> 00:11:54.339
the choice of either saving Zion or going
to rescue Trinity, the Architect reminds us.

00:11:54.339 --> 00:11:58.760
After five monologues on determinism and cause
and effect, I think yes.

00:11:58.760 --> 00:11:59.760
Yes we do.

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:04.420
Neo flies off and saves Trinity, removing
a bullet from her abdomen – surprising no

00:12:04.420 --> 00:12:05.420
one.

00:12:05.420 --> 00:12:10.000
After two hours and eighteen minutes, the
audience is left wondering what they just

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:11.000
watched.

00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:15.779
Sure, a lot more happened on screen than in
the original – there were more fight scenes,

00:12:15.779 --> 00:12:17.709
more characters, more plots twists.

00:12:17.709 --> 00:12:20.460
So why does it feel like nothing actually
happened?

00:12:20.460 --> 00:12:24.050
The answer here lies in our own natural sense
of dramatic action.

00:12:24.050 --> 00:12:28.850
There are only four main plot points here:
Neo finds the Oracle, Neo finds the Key Maker,

00:12:28.850 --> 00:12:32.389
Neo reaches the Source, and Neo saves Trinity.

00:12:32.389 --> 00:12:37.480
These moments are then diluted by an unnecessary
sermons on determinism – sermons, which

00:12:37.480 --> 00:12:41.080
if you can follow them, just remind you that
Neo has no agency.

00:12:41.080 --> 00:12:42.080
All of this is then glued together by long,
unnecessary fight scenes, which have not aged

00:12:42.080 --> 00:12:43.080
well.

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:44.080
And without any dramatic action, the film
leaves us with a few very important and slightly

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:45.080
dull, philosophical questions.

00:12:45.080 --> 00:12:46.750
If all of Neo’s choices are predetermined,
how is anyone supposed to root for him, let

00:12:46.750 --> 00:12:48.519
alone care what happens?

00:12:48.519 --> 00:12:53.040
It seems like the entire point of this 2 hour
18 minute movie is to get the audience to

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:56.180
ask themselves: Can Neo actually make choices?

00:12:56.180 --> 00:13:01.930
Can he break free from the system of control,
the very one we thought he broke free from

00:13:01.930 --> 00:13:03.100
in the first film?

00:13:03.100 --> 00:13:18.329
Instead of answering this, the Matrix Reloaded
tells us to watch the 2 hour long Matrix Revolutions

00:13:18.329 --> 00:13:31.290
to 

00:13:31.290 --> 00:13:42.300
find out…

00:13:42.300 --> 00:13:53.240
Great.

00:13:53.240 --> 00:14:17.300
Just Great.

